%I A400074 #20 Nov 24 2026 12:43:44
%S A400074 4,9,11,16,19,29,31,41,49,59,61,64,71,79,81,89,101,109,121,131,139,
%T A400074 149,151,169,179,181,191,199,211,229,239,241,251,256,269,271,281,289,
%U A400074 311,331,349,359,361,379,389,401,409,419,421,431,439,449,461,479,491,499
%N A400074 Primes powers (A246655) congruent to 1 or 4 mod 5.
%C A400074 Prime powers q such that x^2 + x - 1 (or x^2 - x - 1) is reducible over GF(q).
%C A400074 Union of {p^r : p == 1, 4 (mod 5) prime, r >= 1} and {p^r : p == 2, 3 (mod 5) prime, r >= 2 even}.
%C A400074 The odd terms are exactly the odd prime powers q such that 5 is a nonzero square in the finite field F_q.
%C A400074 By Hensel's lemma, also prime powers q such that 5 is a square in Q_q, where Q_{p^f} is the unique degree-f unramified extension of the p-adic field Q_p. See A085759 for a more general statement.
%H A400074 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400074/b400074.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400074 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hensel%27s_lemma">Hensel's lemma</a>.
%e A400074 5 is a square in Q_4 = Q_2(sqrt(5)).
%e A400074 5 is a square in Q_9 = Q_3(i): 5 = (+-...22110002021211012011*i)^2.
%e A400074 5 is a square in Q_11: 5 = (...8A5A8147937785904A44)^2.
%t A400074 n = 500; r = Prime@ Range@ PrimePi[n]; Union@ Select[r~Join~Flatten@ Table[Array[p^# &, Floor@ Log[p, n] - 1, 2], {p, TakeWhile[r, # <= Sqrt[n] &] } ], MemberQ[{1, 4}, Mod[#, 5] ] &] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Nov 24 2026 *)
%o A400074 (PARI) isA400074(n) = isprimepower(n) && (n%5==1 || n%5==4)
%Y A400074 Union of A327752 and A327753.
%Y A400074 Supersequence of A045468.
%Y A400074 Cf. A246655. See A085759 for related sequences.
%K A400074 nonn,easy,new
%O A400074 1,1
%A A400074 _Jianing Song_, Nov 24 2026
